// A flow that uses the <grammar> markdown tag; open it from Flows.
p({
  "class":"foam.core.reflow.Flow",
  "name":"Grammar tag",
  "category":"GRAMMAR",
  "description":"The <grammar> markdown tag: a grammar written in a document, shown as code or as its railroad diagram.",
  "keywords":["grammar","railroad","parser"],
  "script":"""[
  {
    "flowName": "intro",
    "cmd": "markdown",
    "value": {
      "class": "foam.core.reflow.Markdown",
      "markdown": "# The grammar tag\\n\\nA `foam.parse` grammar written where it is explained. Switch the tab to **Diagram** to see it as a railroad and parse the input.\\n\\n<grammar input=\\"[1, [ab, 22], x]\\">\\n{\\n  START:   alt(seq(sym('list'), eof()), seq(sym('keyword'), eof())),\\n  list:    seq(literal('['), optional(sym('ws')), repeat(sym('item'), seq(optional(sym('ws')), literal(','), optional(sym('ws')))), optional(sym('ws')), literal(']')),\\n  item:    alt(sym('number'), sym('word'), sym('list')),\\n  number:  plus(range('0', '9')),\\n  word:    plus(range('a', 'z')),\\n  ws:      plus(literal(' ')),\\n  keyword: alt(literal('do'), literal('double'))\\n}\\n</grammar>\\n\\nA long input can live in its own block and be named with `input-from`:\\n\\n<grammar input-from=\\"pairs\\">\\n{\\n  START: seq(sym('pair'), repeat(seq(literal(';'), sym('pair'))), eof()),\\n  pair:  seq(sym('key'), literal('='), sym('value')),\\n  key:   plus(range('a', 'z')),\\n  value: plus(notChars(';'))\\n}\\n</grammar>\\n"
    }
  },
  {
    "flowName": "pairs",
    "cmd": "script",
    "value": {
      "class": "foam.core.reflow.Script",
      "autoRun": true,
      "code": "return 'name=alex;city=new york;tz=est';"
    }
  }
]"""
})

// foam3's "Examples Parsers" tutorial flow, with a <grammar> block after each
// parser so every example is also a railroad diagram. Same name as the foam3
// flow, so this entry replaces it at journal merge. Keep in step with
// foam3/src/foam/core/reflow/example/flows.jrl; the diagrams belong upstream.
p({
"class":"foam.core.reflow.Flow",
"name":"Examples Parsers",
"version":3,
"script":
"""[
 {
  "flowName": "markdown1",
  "cmd": "markdown",
  "value": {
   "class": "foam.core.reflow.Markdown",
   "markdown": "# Parsers\\n<pre>\\nFOAM includes parser libraries for both Java and Javascript.\\n\\nThey can be found in the foam.parse package.\\n\\nThe library provides a collection of what are called \\"parse combinators\\", which are simple\\nparsers which can be combined to form larger parsers. While this approach is unusual when\\ncreating parsers, it is a proven technique in other domains, especially in GUI View libraries.\\nA large complex view like an email client or issue tracker is actually composed of simpler\\nviews like buttons, text-fields, tab controls, etc. Parsers can be created by composing\\nsimpler parsers, which are then themselves be composed into larger ones.\\n\\nParsers can be used anywhere a regular-expression could be, but are more powerful and can\\nbe used in places where regular-expressions wouldn't be sufficient. Parsers are generally\\nnot as compact as regular-expressions, but are easier to read.\\n\\nParsers can be used for many things, including:\\n1. extracting data from files\\n2. understanding network requests\\n3. validating and reformatting user input\\n4. creating languages, from small Domain-Specific-Languages (DSLs) to full-featured ones\\n5. source code linters, refactoring tools, or syntax highlighters\\n\\nFOAM parsers accept input in the form of objects which implement the PStream interface.\\nPStream is short for \\"Parser Stream\\".\\n\\n\\nPStreams have the following interface:\\n    get int     pos   - The character position in the input stream.\\n\\n    get Char    head  - The first character in the stream at the current position.\\n\\n    get PStream tail  - A PStream for the next position in the input stream.\\n\\n    get Object  value - 'Value' associated with this PStream.\\n\\n    PStream setValue(Object value) - Creates a new PStream at the same position\\n        but with a new 'value'. The value is used to hold the result of a\\n        (sub-)parse.\\n\\nPStreams are immutable, which greatly simplifies backtracking.\\n\\nA Parser has the following interface:\\n    PStream parse(PStream stream);\\n\\nIt takes a PStream as input, and returns either a PStream\\nadvanced to the point after all input is consumed by the parser,\\nor 'undefined' if the parse failed. The value generated by the parser\\nis stored in the .value property of the returned PStream.\\n</pre>\\n\\n## Simple Parsers\\nFirst we'll start off by explaining the \\"simple\\" parsers. Simple parsers do not combine\\nany other parsers, they do all of their parsing on their own.\\n\\n\\n## foam.parse.Parsers\\nWe use the foam.parse.Parsers Singleton class which provides convenience constructors\\nfor the various parser types.\\n\\n\\n## Literal Parser\\nThe simplest parser is a literal parser, which only parses the supplied string.\\n<example>\\n// Create a Singleton for accessing the built-in Parser library\\nvar P = foam.parse.Parsers.create();\\n\\n// Create an input PStream to parse our String\\nvar ps = foam.parse.StringPStream.create({str: 'abcdef'});\\n\\n// Let's look at our PStream\\nlog(ps.toString());\\n\\n// Now create a Literal Parser using the Singleton instance 'P' created above.\\n// This parser will only parse the literal 'abc'.\\nvar lp = P.literal('abc');\\n\\n// Now let's parse the PStream with the Parser\\nps = lp.parse(ps);\\n\\n// Now let's look at the results of our parsing\\nlog(ps.toString());\\n\\n// If we try to parse 'abc' again notice that it will fail because the stream is now\\n// at 'def', since 'abc' was consumed.\\nvar np = lp.parse(ps);\\nlog(!np?'undefined':np.toString());\\n\\n// Here we'll create an in-lined literal 'def' parser and then call in directly.\\nlog(P.literal('def').parse(ps).toString());</example>\\nEvery parser below is also drawn as a railroad diagram: switch its tab to **Diagram** and press **Parse** to watch it run on the input.\\n\\n<grammar input=\\"abcdef\\">\\n{ START: literal('abc') }\\n</grammar>\\n\\n\\n## Parser Tester\\nBefore continuing, let's write a helper function testP() to make demonstrating parser examples easier.\\n\\nThe testP() function uses the U2 GUI library to create a table of input output pairs.\\nFor more information on the U2 library, please view the U2 examples. For our purpose though,\\nyou can treat this function as a blackbox that takes a parser and an array of strings to parse as input\\nand displays the results in a table\\n<example>\\n// Create a Singleton for accessing the built-in Parser library\\nconst P = foam.parse.Parsers.create();\\n\\n// Function that invokes the parser for the given array of strs\\nfunction testP(parser, strs) {\\n  globalThis.scope.br().start('table').\\n    style({'min-width': '90%'}).\\n    attrs({border: 1, cellpadding: 6, cellspacing: 0}).\\n    start('tr').start('th').\\n      style({background: '#eee'}).\\n      attrs({colspan:3}).add(`Parser: ${parser}`)\\n    .end().\\n  end().\\n  start('tr').\\n    start('th').\\n      add('Input').\\n    end().\\n    start('th').\\n      add('Parsed').\\n    end().\\n    start('th').\\n      add('Result').\\n    end().\\n  end().\\n  forEach(strs, function(str) {\\n    // create a mutable slot for the input, this way we can change our examples on the fly\\n    var inputSlot  = foam.lang.SimpleSlot.create({value: str});\\n    // create another mutable slot to hold the parser output\\n    var outputSlot = inputSlot.map(input => parser.parse(P.stringPStream(input)));\\n\\n    this.start('tr').\\n      start('td').\\n        style({width:'25%'}).\\n        tag({class: 'foam.u2.TextField', size: 10, onKey: true, data$: inputSlot}).\\n      end().\\n      start('td').start('pre').\\n        add(outputSlot.map(output => output ? str.substring(0, output.pos) : '')).\\n      end().end().\\n      start('td').start('pre').\\n        style({color: outputSlot.map(output => output ? 'green' : 'red')}).\\n        add(outputSlot.map(output => output ? JSON.stringify(output.value) : 'undefined')).\\n      end().end().\\n    end();\\n  });\\n}\\n\\n// Demonstrate the testP() function\\ntestP(P.literal('abc'), [ 'abc', 'abcdef', 'Abc', 'ABC', 'xyz' ]);\\n\\n// Store the test function and the singleton for the rest of the examples\\nglobalThis.P = P;\\nglobalThis.testP = testP;</example>\\n\\n## StringPStreams\\nThere's also a convenience method for creating StringPStreams in foam.parse.Parsers.\\nNotice that the two StringPStreams appear identical, even though they are created in\\ndifferent ways.\\n<example>\\nvar ps = foam.parse.StringPStream.create({str: 'abc'});\\nlog(ps.toString());\\n\\n// Using the 'P' Singleton we created above.\\nlog(P.stringPStream('abc').toString());</example>\\n\\n## Literal Ignore Case\\nThe literalIC() parser is like literal(), that we saw earlier, but it performs a\\ncase-insensitive match.\\nNotice that it also parses 'Abc' and 'ABC', whereas the literal() didn't.\\n<example>\\ntestP(P.literalIC('abc'), [ 'abc', 'abcdef', 'Abc', 'ABC', 'xyz' ]);</example>\\n<grammar input=\\"Abc\\">\\n{ START: literalIC('abc') }\\n</grammar>\\n\\n\\n## Any Char\\nThe anyChar() parser is the most indiscriminating parser. It will parse any one character.\\nNotice that the only case where it fails to parse is when given an empty input.\\n<example>\\ntestP(P.anyChar(), [ 'abc', 'ABC', '!@#$%', '' ]);</example>\\n<grammar input=\\"abc\\">\\n{ START: anyChar() }\\n</grammar>\\n\\n\\n## EOF\\nEOF is a standard acronym for End Of File, but more generally it means the end of\\nany input source.\\nIt is, in a way, the oppose of the anyChar() parser, since it won't parse any character\\nexcept the EOF.\\n<example>\\ntestP(P.eof(), [ 'abc', 'ABC', '!@#$%', '' ]);</example>\\n<grammar input=\\"abc\\">\\n{ START: seq(literal('abc'), eof()) }\\n</grammar>\\n\\n\\n## Chars Parser\\nThe chars() parser will parse any single character which is included as part of the\\nsupplied string.\\n<example>\\ntestP(P.chars('abc'), [ 'ax', 'bx', 'cx', 'xb', '' ]);</example>\\n<grammar input=\\"ax\\">\\n{ START: chars('abc') }\\n</grammar>\\n\\n\\n## Not Chars Parser\\nThe notChars() parser will parse any single character which is NOT included as part of the\\nsupplied string. However, it will not parse EOF.\\n<example>\\ntestP(P.notChars('abc'), [ 'ax', 'bx', 'cx', 'xb', '' ]);</example>\\n<grammar input=\\"xb\\">\\n{ START: notChars('abc') }\\n</grammar>\\n\\n\\n## Range Parser\\nThe range() parser is similar to chars(), except that it lets you more easily specify a range of characters.\\n<example>\\ntestP(P.range('0', '9'), [ 'a', '1', '2', '3', '9', 'z' ]);\\n\\n// Is short form for:\\ntestP(P.chars('0123456789'), [ 'a', '1', '2', '3', '9', 'z' ]);\\n\\n// It is often used to detect numbers (like above) or letters, like:\\ntestP(P.range('a', 'z'), [ 'a', '1', '2', '3', '9', 'z' ]);\\n\\n// Notice that it still only matches a single character\\ntestP(P.range('a', 'z'), [ 'abc', '123' ]);</example>\\n<grammar input=\\"7\\">\\n{ START: range('0', '9') }\\n</grammar>\\n\\n\\n## Combinators\\nThe above parsers are all simple parsers. They all parse a single character or a literal string.\\nNow we'll look at combining the above simple parsers with parse combinators to create more complex parsers.\\n<example></example>\\n\\n## Sequence Parser\\nThe most common parse combinator is the sequence parser, which combines multiple parsers\\ninto a single parser which only parses if each of the composed parsers successfully parses\\nin sequence. Each parser picks up where the previous parser left off in the input stream.\\nThe sequence parser will fail if any of the composed parsers fails to parse.\\nOn a successful parse, the returned value is an array of the parsed values of each\\ncomposed parser.\\n<example>\\n// The first parser accepts a letter and the second parser accepts a number.\\nvar p = P.seq(P.range('a','z'), P.range('0','9'));\\ntestP(p, [ 'r2', 'd2', '3p', 'xy' ]);</example>\\n<grammar input=\\"r2\\">\\n{ START: seq(range('a', 'z'), range('0', '9')) }\\n</grammar>\\n\\n\\n## Literal Parser Shortcut\\nIn the example below, notice that we have three calls to P.literal(). Since P.literal() is so\\ncommonly used when creating parsers, we make it the default when you just pass a String\\nto another parse combinator, like seq().\\n<example>\\nconst TESTS = [ 'axxx', 'abxxx', 'abcxxx' ];\\n\\n// The longer explicit method:\\ntestP(P.seq(P.literal('a'), P.literal('b'), P.literal('c')), TESTS);\\n\\n// A shorter more readable method:\\ntestP(P.seq('a', 'b', 'c'), TESTS);</example>\\n\\n## String Parser\\nIn one of the previous examples, when we demonstrated the sequence parser seq(), parsing 'r2' resulted\\n in the parsed value of `[ 'r', '2' ]`, an array. However, we often\\nwant the result of our parsers to be string values, not the arrays of characters or\\nsubstrings that were parsed by the individual parsers that comprised the sequence parser.\\nTo facilitate this, we use a very simple combinator parser called str() that decorates a single parser.\\nInstead of changing that delegate parser's behaviour, it just\\nchanges its resulting value so that if it returns an array, it converts it into a single\\nstring value with the array values concatenated.\\n<example>\\n// Notice unlike above, our results are strings, not arrays\\nvar p = P.seq(P.range('a','z'), P.range('0','9'));\\ntestP(P.str(p), [ 'r2', 'd2', '3p', 'xy' ]);</example>\\n<grammar input=\\"r2\\">\\n{ START: str(seq(range('a', 'z'), range('0', '9'))) }\\n</grammar>\\n\\n\\n## Substring Parser\\n<pre>\\nThe String parser shown above takes an array of parsed string values an concatenates them\\ninto a single string return value. However, it is often the case that this isn't actually\\nwhat you want. Consider two cases where using str() is undesirable:\\n1. Sometimes you have multiple levels of string arrays and you would need to insert a str()\\nat each level.\\n2. You want to preserve input that has been lost, like the delimiters in a repeat().\\n\\nConsider the following example which parses a sentence, which is a list of words separated\\nby whitespace. If you wanted to return the parsed string you would need to add a str()\\naround the 'word' parser so that you get strings instead of arrays of characters and then\\nyou would need to add another str() around the repeat() in 'sentence'. But even this wouldn't\\nwork because repeat() would have removed the whitespaces.\\n\\nTo solve this problem there is the substring() parse combinator which just returns the input\\nstring that was parsed, as is, ignoring the return value of the composed parser.\\n\\n???: perhaps input() would be a better name than substring()\\n</pre>\\n<example>\\nvar   word       = P.str(P.plus(P.range('a','z')));\\nconst whitespace = P.plus(' ');\\nconst sentence   = P.repeat(word, whitespace);\\n\\nconst input = [ 'abc', 'abc def', 'abc   def   xyz' ];\\n\\n// returns an array of words\\ntestP(sentence, input);\\n\\n// returns a single string of the words concatenated, without spaces\\ntestP(P.str(sentence), input);\\n\\n// preserves the original input\\ntestP(P.substring(sentence), input);\\n\\n// Notice that the str() on 'word' isn't needed when using substring().\\nvar   word       = P.plus(P.range('a','z'));\\ntestP(P.substring(sentence), input);</example>\\n<grammar input=\\"abc   def   xyz\\">\\n{\\n  START: substring(sym('sentence')),\\n  sentence: repeat(sym('word'), sym('whitespace')),\\n  word: plus(range('a', 'z')),\\n  whitespace: plus(literal(' '))\\n}\\n</grammar>\\n\\n\\n## Alternate Parser\\nThe next most common parse combinator after the sequence parser, is the alt() parser.\\nIt's job is to take multiple delgate parsers, just like seq(), but to succeed if any of them\\nsucceed. It tries parsing with each composed parser in turn, and returns the result of the\\nfirst one that succeeds.\\n<example>\\ntestP(P.alt(P.literal('A'), P.literal('B')), [ 'A', 'B', 'C' ]);\\n\\n// Or just:\\n\\ntestP(P.alt('A', 'B'), [ 'A', 'B', 'C' ]);</example>\\n<grammar input=\\"B\\">\\n{ START: alt(literal('A'), literal('B')) }\\n</grammar>\\n\\n\\n## Repeat\\nThe repeat() parser takes a single delegate parser and repeatedly uses it to parse\\nthe input stream until it fails. The result of all delegate parses are combined in an array.\\n\\n<example>\\ntestP(P.repeat(P.range('0','9')), [ '1', '1x', '123xyz', 'abc' ]);\\n\\n// Same example with the values converted into strings\\ntestP(P.str(P.repeat(P.range('0','9'))), [ '1', '1x', '123xyz', 'abc' ]);</example>\\n<grammar input=\\"123xyz\\">\\n{ START: repeat(range('0', '9')) }\\n</grammar>\\n\\n\\n## Plus\\nYou might have noticed that in the above example, repeat(0-9) succeeded in parsing the string 'abc' and provided an empty string result \\"\\" since there was no match. This is because repeat() parses zero or more occurrences of the delegate parser, so failure is a valid option. However, it's often the\\ncase that you want to consider a repeated parser to have succeeded when it parses one or more\\ntimes. For this, there's the plus() parser, which behaves just like repeat(), but fails in\\nthe case where the delegate parses 0 times.\\n<example>\\ntestP(P.str(P.plus(P.range('0','9'))), [ '1', '1x', '123xyz', 'abc' ]);</example>\\n<grammar input=\\"123xyz\\">\\n{ START: str(plus(range('0', '9'))) }\\n</grammar>\\n\\n\\n## Delimited Repeat\\nIt's common to want to use repeat() to parse some pattern where each entry is separated\\nby some other pattern. Examples include a comma separated list or semi-colon separated\\nprogram statements. While you could do this by including the delimiter with seq() in the\\ndelegate parser, this is so common an operation that repeat supports it directly.\\n<example>\\n// First lets' look how we would parse a comma delimited list of letters without using this feature:\\ntestP(\\n  P.str(P.repeat(P.str(P.seq(P.range('a','z'), P.literal(','))))),\\n  [ 'a', 'a,b', 'a,b,c', 'a,b,c,'\\n]);\\n\\n\\n// Notice that the above never parsed the last letter, except for the last case: 'a,b,c,'\\n// because there's a bug in our parser. We only want the comma between letters, not after,\\n// which prevents the last 'c' from being parsed.\\n// To fix this, we need to make our parser more complicated:\\ntestP(\\n  P.str(P.seq(P.str(P.repeat(P.str(P.seq(P.range('a','z'), P.literal(','))))), P.range('a', 'z'))),\\n  [ 'a', 'a,b', 'a,b,c', 'a,b,c,'\\n]);\\n\\n// This fixes that problem, but our parser is getting more complicated. Also,\\n// notice that it is including the commas in the parsed output, but we probably\\n// just want the letters.\\n// The solution is to specify the delimiter to repeat():\\ntestP(\\n  P.str(P.repeat(P.range('a','z'), ',')),\\n  [ '', 'a', 'a,b', 'a,b,c', 'a,b,c,'\\n]);\\n\\n// By supplying the delimiter parser as the second argument to repeat(), it knows that we\\n// only want to skip delimiters between values and it excludes them from the output.\\n// It is also shorter and easier to understand than our first attempt.\\n\\n// You can also specify a delimiter parser with plus(), in cases where you only want\\n// the parse to succeed when you have one or more parses.\\ntestP(\\n P.str(P.plus(P.range('a','z'), ',')),\\n  [ '', 'a', 'a,b', 'a,b,c', 'a,b,c,'\\n]);</example>\\n<grammar input=\\"a,b,c\\">\\n{ START: str(repeat(range('a', 'z'), literal(','))) }\\n</grammar>\\n\\n\\n## Repeat with a Number of Repetitions\\nThe repeat() parses zero or more times and plus() repeats one or more times, but sometimes\\nyou want to parse with some minimum number of repeats other than zero or one.\\nThe third optional argument of repeat() let's you specify just that.\\nSince the second argument is the delimiter parser, if you don't want\\nto have a delimiter, pass 'undefined' as its value.\\n\\nThe below example checks passwords to ensure that they're at least five characters long.\\nNotice that the repeat() parser still continues until it fails, hence for the last sample\\nit repeated six times, meaning that the specified repeat parameter is the miniumum number\\nof repetitions not the absolute limit.\\n<example>\\ntestP(\\n  P.str(P.repeat(P.alt(P.range('a','z'), P.range('A', 'Z'), P.range('0', '9'), P.anyChar('!@#$%^&*')), undefined, 5, 7)),\\n  [ 's', 's@', 's@c', 's@C&', 's@C&t', 's@C&t1', 's@C&t123456' ]\\n);</example>\\n<grammar input=\\"s@C&t1\\">\\n{ START: str(repeat(alt(range('a', 'z'), range('A', 'Z'), range('0', '9'), chars('!@#$%^&*')), undefined, 5, 7)) }\\n</grammar>\\n\\n\\n## Not Parser\\nThe Not parser is unusual in that it suceeds only if it's delegate parser fails.\\n<example>\\nvar quote        = P.literal('\\"');\\nvar stringBody   = P.str(P.repeat(P.not(quote, P.anyChar())));\\nvar stringParser = P.seq(quote, stringBody, quote);\\ntestP(P.str(stringParser), [ '\\"\\"', '\\"a\\"', '\\"abc13234$@#$\\"', '\\"abc\\"asfasdf\\"' ]);</example>\\n<grammar>\\n{\\n  START: seq(sym('quote'), sym('body'), sym('quote')),\\n  quote: literal('\\"'),\\n  body: str(repeat(not(sym('quote'), anyChar())))\\n}\\n</grammar>\\n\\n\\n## Seq1 Parser\\nNotice that in the previous string parser example, our result value was an array where\\nthe first and last elements were the quote '\\"' characters, and the second element\\nwas the actual string value we wanted. We would like the output value to ignore the\\nsurrounding syntax and just return us the one inner value that we're interested in.\\nThis is common enough to warrant it's own parse combinator. Seq1 behaves just like the regular\\nseq() parser except instead of returning an array of values parsed by the delegates, it only\\nreturns the return value of one parser, the index of which you specify as the first argument.\\nBy using seq1(1, ...) instead of seq(...) we tell the parser to ignore the quote parsers and\\njust give us the result of the stringBody parser, which is at location 1 (starting at zero).\\n<example>\\nvar quote        = P.literal('\\"');\\nvar stringBody   = P.str(P.repeat(P.not(quote, P.anyChar())));\\nvar stringParser = P.seq1(1, quote, stringBody, quote);\\ntestP(stringParser, [ '\\"\\"', '\\"a\\"', '\\"abc13234$@#$\\"', '\\"abc\\"asfasdf\\"' ]);</example>\\n<grammar>\\n{\\n  START: seq1(1, sym('quote'), sym('body'), sym('quote')),\\n  quote: literal('\\"'),\\n  body: str(repeat(not(sym('quote'), anyChar())))\\n}\\n</grammar>\\n\\n\\n## Until Parser\\nThe string pattern in the above example combined repeat(), not(), and anyChar() in order to extract the string.\\nThis pattern is common enough that we have a special parser for that called until().\\nIt parses input until the delegated parser matches.\\nWe can use it to simplify the above stringParser.\\n<example>\\nvar quote        = P.literal('\\"');\\nvar stringParser = P.str(P.seq1(1, quote, P.until(quote)));\\ntestP(stringParser, [ '\\"\\"', '\\"a\\"', '\\"abc13234$@#$\\"', '\\"abc\\"asfasdf\\"' ]);</example>\\n<grammar>\\n{ START: str(seq1(1, literal('\\"'), until(literal('\\"')))) }\\n</grammar>\\n\\n\\n## Optional Parser\\nThe optional() parser composes a single delegate parser and if that parser parses, then it returns\\nits result, otherwise it returns the PStream as is, without failing. This lets parsing\\ncontinue from the same point.\\n<example>\\ntestP(P.seq(P.optional('header'), 'body'),\\n  [ 'headerbody', 'body', 'header' ]\\n);</example>\\n<grammar input=\\"headerbody\\">\\n{ START: seq(optional(literal('header')), literal('body')) }\\n</grammar>\\n\\n\\n## Example\\nThe following example uses optional() several times to create a phone number parser where\\nthe long distance '1', the area code, and some punctuation are all optional.\\nNotice that the parser also uses the str() combinator in several spots to ensure the final\\noutput is a string rather than an array.\\nNotice this line: P.optional(P.literal('-'), '-'),\\nWe can specify a second parameter to optional() that returns the value to be used if the parser\\ndoesn't parse. In this case, we just want the add '-' if it wasn't present, to standardize\\nthe output format of the parser.\\n<example>\\n\\nvar digit    = P.range('0', '9');\\nvar areaCode = P.str(P.seq('(', digit, digit, digit, ')'));\\nvar phoneNumber = P.str(P.seq(\\n  P.optional('1'),\\n  P.optional(' '),\\n  P.optional(areaCode),\\n  P.optional(' ', ' '),\\n  P.str(P.seq(digit, digit, digit)),\\n  P.optional('-', '-'),\\n  P.str(P.seq(digit, digit, digit, digit))\\n));\\n\\ntestP(phoneNumber, [\\n  '1 (555) 555-1234',\\n  '1(555) 555-1234',\\n  '(555) 555-1234',\\n  '(555)555-1234',\\n  '555-1234',\\n  '5551234'\\n]);</example>\\n<grammar input=\\"1 (555) 555-1234\\">\\n{\\n  START: sym('phoneNumber'),\\n  digit: range('0', '9'),\\n  areaCode: str(seq('(', sym('digit'), sym('digit'), sym('digit'), ')')),\\n  phoneNumber: str(seq(optional('1'), optional(' '), optional(sym('areaCode')), optional(' '), str(seq(sym('digit'), sym('digit'), sym('digit'))), optional('-'), str(seq(sym('digit'), sym('digit'), sym('digit'), sym('digit')))))\\n}\\n</grammar>\\n\\n\\n## Optimizations\\n## Repeat0\\n<pre>\\nSome parsers end with the suffix '0'. This means they are specialized to not return any\\nvalue. This may seem unnecessary, and from a strict feature point of view, it is, but\\nthe advantage of this type of parser is that it is more efficient because it performs\\nless work and generates less garbage. Parser performance can be very important because\\nit is often the most expensive operation performed by many applications.\\n\\nIn this example we use the repeat0() parser to parse whitespace. We don't care about\\nthe whitespace, so we don't really care about the size or formation of the whitespace\\nand just want to skip over it efficiently. This is common for programming languages\\nwhere a significant portion of the input may be whitespace, and yet we don't need to extract\\nany values from the whitespace. We just want to skip over it as quickly and efficiently\\nas possible.\\n</pre>\\n<example>\\nconst whitespace = P.repeat0(P.chars(' \\\\t\\\\n'), null, 1);\\nconst word       = P.str(P.plus(P.range('a', 'z')));\\nconst words      = P.repeat(word, whitespace);\\n\\ntestP(words, [\\n  'abc',\\n  'word word   abc     xyz',\\n  `word    word\\n   on another line\\n   and more\\n  `\\n]);</example>\\n<grammar input=\\"word word   abc\\">\\n{\\n  START: repeat(sym('word'), sym('whitespace')),\\n  whitespace: repeat0(chars(' \\\\t\\\\n'), null, 1),\\n  word: str(plus(range('a', 'z')))\\n}\\n</grammar>\\n\\n\\n## Seq0 and Until0\\nJust as repeat0() is the no-value version of repeat(), seq0() is the no-value version of seq() and until0() is the no-value version of until().\\nWe use both seq0() and until0() in this example to parse C-style `/* comments */`. For a language compiler we might throw away the contents of\\ncomments, so this type of parser might make sense.\\n<example>\\nvar comment = P.seq0('/*', P.until0('*/'));\\ntestP(comment, [ '/* This is a comment. Ignore the results. */' ]);\\n\\n// If you wanted the comment contents, then you would do:\\n\\nvar comment = P.seq1(1, '/*', P.str(P.until('*/')));\\ntestP(comment, [ \\"/* This is a comment. Don't ignore the results. */\\" ]);\\n</example>\\n<grammar input=\\"/* a comment */\\">\\n{ START: seq0('/*', until0('*/')) }\\n</grammar>\\n\\n\\n## Example: Boolean\\nConsider the following simple parser which is meant to parse boolean true or false values:\\n<example>\\nvar booleanParser1 = P.alt('true', 'false');\\ntestP(booleanParser1, [ 'true', 'false' ]);\\n\\n// The above booleanParser1 works, but isn't ideal because the values it returns are\\n// the string values \\"true\\" and \\"false\\", but since we are parsing booleans, we would\\n// probably prefer getting back the actual boolean true and false values (ie. actual booleans\\n// not strings).\\n\\n// This second version is almost the same but it specifies a second optional argument\\n// to literal() which substitutes the value to be returned instead of the specified\\n// string literal, in the event that the parse succeeds.\\n\\n\\nvar booleanParser2 = P.alt(P.literal('true', true), P.literal('false', false));\\ntestP(booleanParser2, [ 'true', 'false' ]);\\n\\n// Now we get actual true and false values rather than \\"true\\" and \\"false\\".</example>\\n<grammar input=\\"false\\">\\n{ START: alt('true', 'false') }\\n</grammar>\\n\\n\\n## Grammars: Parenthesis Matching\\n<pre>\\nAll of the above examples could have also been done with regular-expressions, but this\\nis the first example of something which can be parsed by a general-purpose parser but\\ncould not be parsed with a regular-expression.\\n\\nIn this example we detect if a group of parenthesis is matched. To do so, the definition\\nneeds to be recursive, which cannot be done with regular expressions. Notice that the\\ndefinition of 'parens' makes reference to itself with the sym('parens') parser.\\n\\nWhen making recursive or more complex parsers, it is common to combine the individual\\nparsers into what is called a 'Grammar'. A grammar is a collection of mutually recursive\\nnamed parsers which together form a higher-level parser. A grammar is similar to how\\nmany methods are combined in a single class in OO programming. Because grammars and OO\\nclasses are so similar, FOAM uses classes to represent grammars.\\n\\nTo create a Grammar, you create a foam.CLASS which extends 'foam.parse.Grammar' and then\\ncreate a 'grammar' method which returns the grammar as JS map. The name you give each\\nparser in the map you return is called its symbol name. In the example below these are 'START' and 'parens'.\\nYou reference a named parser using the sym() parser, like sym('parens'), in the example.\\nIn that case, it is referencing itself. The symbol named 'START' is special because it\\nrepresents the default top-level parser for your grammar. When we use the MatchedParensParser,\\nit is the START parser which is used.\\n\\nNotice that any of the parsers described previously are made available to the grammar\\nmethod as parameters. This is done to minimize syntax and make the resulting grammar easier\\nto read.\\n\\nParser grammars are normally defined in <a href=\\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Extended_Backus%E2%80%93Naur_form\\">Extended Backus–Naur form (EBNF)</a>, which is similar\\nenough to FOAM Grammars to make conversion easy.\\n\\n</pre>\\n<example>\\nfoam.CLASS({\\n  name: 'MatchedParensParser',\\n  extends: 'foam.parse.Grammar',\\n\\n  methods: [\\n    function grammar(str, sym, eof, repeat, seq, seq1) {\\n      return {\\n        START: seq1(0, sym('parens'), eof()),\\n\\n        // The 'repeat' allows for ()'s to be side-by-side, like ()() or (()())\\n        parens: str(repeat(\\n          str(seq('(', sym('parens'), ')')),\\n        ))\\n      };\\n    }\\n  ]\\n});\\n\\nvar p = MatchedParensParser.create();\\n\\ntestP(p, [\\n  // Matched\\n  '', '()', '(())', '()()', '((())()(()))',\\n  // Unmatched\\n  ')(', '(()', '())'\\n]);\\n\\n</example>\\n<grammar input=\\"((())()(()))\\">\\n{\\n  START: seq1(0, sym('parens'), eof()),\\n  parens: str(repeat(str(seq('(', sym('parens'), ')'))))\\n}\\n</grammar>\\n\\n\\n## Grammars: Phone Number Parser\\nWhile the above Grammar had only a single rule 'parens', it is more common for a grammar\\nto have multiple rules, as in this example which implements a telephone number parser.\\n<example>\\nfoam.CLASS({\\n  name: 'PhoneNumberParser',\\n  extends: 'foam.parse.Grammar',\\n\\n  methods: [\\n    function grammar(alt, sym, seq1, seq, str, literal, optional, plus, range) {\\n      return {\\n        START: str(seq(\\n          optional(sym('countryCode')),\\n          alt(\\n            str(seq(sym('areaCode'), optional(' ', ' '), sym('simple'))),\\n            sym('simple'))\\n        )),\\n\\n        simple: str(seq(sym('prefix'), optional(sym('separator'), '-'), sym('line'))),\\n\\n        digit: range('0', '9'),\\n\\n        countryCode: seq1(1, optional('+'), literal('1', '1 '), optional(' ')),\\n\\n        areaCode: str(seq(\\n          optional('(', '('),\\n          sym('digit'), sym('digit'), sym('digit'),\\n          optional(')', ')'))),\\n\\n        prefix: str(seq( sym('digit'), sym('digit'), sym('digit') )),\\n\\n        separator: alt('-', ' '),\\n\\n        line: str(seq( sym('digit'), sym('digit'), sym('digit'), sym('digit') ))\\n      };\\n    }\\n  ]\\n});\\n\\nvar p = PhoneNumberParser.create();\\n\\n// A good way to test/debug Grammars to to test each symbol individually:\\n\\ntestP(p.getSymParser('countryCode'), [ '', '1', '+1', '+1 ' ]);\\ntestP(p.getSymParser('areaCode'),    [ '', '555', '(555)', '55' ]);\\ntestP(p.getSymParser('prefix'),      [ '', '12', '123', '1234' ]);\\ntestP(p.getSymParser('line'),        [ '', '123', '1234', '12345' ]);\\n\\ntestP(p, [\\n  '+1 (555) 555-1234',\\n  '1 (555) 555-1234',\\n  '1(555) 555-1234',\\n  '(555) 555-1234',\\n  '555 555-1234',\\n  '15555551234',\\n  '5555551234',\\n  '(555)555-1234',\\n  '555-1234',\\n  '5551234'\\n]);</example>\\n<grammar input=\\"+1 (555) 555-1234\\">\\n{\\n  START: str(seq(optional(sym('countryCode')), alt(str(seq(sym('areaCode'), optional(' ', ' '), sym('simple'))), sym('simple')))),\\n  simple: str(seq(sym('prefix'), optional(sym('separator'), '-'), sym('line'))),\\n  digit: range('0', '9'),\\n  countryCode: seq1(1, optional('+'), literal('1', '1 '), optional(' ')),\\n  areaCode: str(seq(optional('(', '('), sym('digit'), sym('digit'), sym('digit'), optional(')', ')'))),\\n  prefix: str(seq(sym('digit'), sym('digit'), sym('digit'))),\\n  separator: alt('-', ' '),\\n  line: str(seq(sym('digit'), sym('digit'), sym('digit'), sym('digit')))\\n}\\n</grammar>\\n\\n\\n## Semantic Actions\\n<pre>\\nSo far all of our parsers have mostly just returned the matched input but haven't actually\\nconverted the input into anything else. While this is what is commonly done with\\nregular-expressions, parsers typically convert the input into something useful.\\nFor an example a parser for the language 'C' might convert the source code input\\ninto an Abstract Syntax Tree (AST) that is used by later stages of the compiler,\\nor a calculator expression parser might convert an arithmetic expression into\\nthe result of that calculation.\\n\\nThis is done by what are called \\"Semantic Actions\\", which are just map functions which\\nare applied to the parser result to transform it into some other value.\\n\\nBelow is a very simple example which only adds one semantic action to our Grammar.\\nIn this case it adds an action to the 'number' symbol so that numbers are converted\\nto actual JS numbers, rather than being left as Strings.\\n\\nSemantic Actions are defined by creating a method named &lt;SymbolName&gt;Action. So in this\\nexample, the 'numberAction' method is applied to results parsed by the 'number' parser.\\n</pre>\\n<example>\\n\\nfoam.CLASS({\\n  name: 'VersionParser',\\n  extends: 'foam.parse.Grammar',\\n\\n  methods: [\\n    function grammar(alt, not, sym, seq, str, optional, plus, repeat, range, anyChar) {\\n      return {\\n        START: repeat(sym('component'), optional(sym('separator'))),\\n\\n        component: alt(sym('number'), sym('string')),\\n\\n        digit: range('0', '9'),\\n\\n        number: str(plus(sym('digit'))),\\n\\n        string: str(plus(not(alt(sym('digit'), sym('separator')), anyChar()))),\\n\\n        separator: alt('.', '-')\\n      };\\n    },\\n\\n    function numberAction(v) { return parseInt(v); }\\n  ]\\n});\\n\\n\\nvar p = VersionParser.create();\\n\\ntestP(p, [ '1', '1.1', '1.1b', '3.14159-26b' ]);</example>\\n\\n## Email Address Parser\\nThe following parser uses two semantic actions to parse email addresses which can be\\neither simple like \\"john@email.com\\" or labelled like \\"John Doe &lt;john@email.com&gt;\\".\\n\\n<!--\\nTODO: Doesn't parse for some reason.\\n<example>\\n\\nfoam.CLASS({\\n  name: 'EmailAddressParser',\\n  extends: 'foam.parse.Grammar',\\n\\n  methods: [\\n    function grammar(alt, sym, seq, str, repeat, notChars) {\\n      return {\\n        START: sym('address'),\\n\\n        addressList: repeat(sym('address'), seq(',', repeat(' '))),\\n\\n        address: alt(sym('labelledAddress'), sym('simpleAddress')),\\n\\n        labelledAddress: seq(repeat(notChars('<,')), '<', sym('simpleAddress'), '>'),\\n\\n        simpleAddress: seq(repeat(notChars('@')), '@', repeat(notChars('\\\\r>,')))\\n      };\\n    },\\n\\n    function labelledAddressAction(v) { return v[0].join('') + v[1] + v[2] + v[3]; },\\n\\n    function simpleAddressAction(v) { return v[0].join('') + v[1] + v[2].join(''); }\\n  ]\\n});\\n\\nvar p = EmailAddressParser.create();\\n\\ntestP(p, [ 'John Doe<John@email.com>', 'john@email.com' ]);\\n\\n// Notice we also added an 'addressList' rule to the grammar, which is useful:\\ntestP(p.getSymParser('addressList'), [ 'john@email.com', 'john@email.com,jane@email.com' ]);</example>\\n<grammar input=\\"john@email.com\\">\\n{\\n  START: sym('address'),\\n  addressList: repeat(sym('address'), seq(',', repeat(' '))),\\n  address: alt(sym('labelledAddress'), sym('simpleAddress')),\\n  labelledAddress: seq(repeat(notChars('<,')), '<', sym('simpleAddress'), '>'),\\n  simpleAddress: seq(repeat(notChars('@')), '@', repeat(notChars('\\\\r>,')))\\n}\\n</grammar>\\n -->\\n\\n## Match and MatchAll\\n<pre>\\nUsually parsers consume the entire input, but they can also be used like regexes to find\\none or multiple instances in a larger input. Calling .match() on a Grammar will find the first\\ninstance in the input text, whereas matchAll() will return an array of all matches.\\n\\nThe following example uses the previous PhoneNumberParser to extract one or more phone numbers\\nfrom an input text.\\n</pre>\\n<example>\\nvar p = PhoneNumberParser.create();\\nvar input = 'blah blah blah (555) 123-4556, +1 (555) 123-1234 blah blah blah 555-1234  blah blah';\\nlog('One:', p.match(input));\\nlog('All:', p.matchAll(input).join(', '));</example>\\n\\n## Grammar Reuse\\n<pre>\\nTypical parser generators like YACC (Yet Another Compiler Compiler) do not offer very good\\nmechanisms for reuse, but Object-Oriented (OO) programmers are used to having <b>inheritance</b> and <b>composition</b>\\nfor this purpose. Inheritance allows a class to derive properties and behavior from a parent class,\\nenabling code reuse and specialization. Composition, on the other hand, involves building complex\\nfunctionality by combining simpler, independent components, promoting modularity and flexibility.\\n\\nFOAM Grammars nicely bring the worlds of high-level parser specifications and OO together,\\nso that you can have the advantages of modern OO languages while writing your grammars.\\n\\nThis example demonstrates reuse through both composition and inheritance. The example is of a\\nsimple expression parser and interpreter derived from the built-in base defined in:\\n<a href=\\"https://github.com/foam-framework/foam/blob/2966cde661ccadbb8b1548fede78f6d67cc53ed5/core/experimental/scaleParser.js\\">scaleParser.js</a>.\\n\\nFor <b>composition</b>, we create a NumberParser which is only able to parse negative or positive\\nintegers. This class/grammar isn't very large, but by the time we add support for\\ndecimal places, exponents, and maybe even numbers expressed in different bases, this class\\ncould become fairly large. It makes the code more modular and reusable to keep isolated\\nfeatures in their own classes. This way we could reuse the NumberParser in other classes,\\nor could have different versions (say a JS version or a Java version) and then\\nchoose between implementations if we wanted to support JS or Java style\\nnumbers.\\n\\nTo illustrate <b>inheritance</b>, we first create a generic ExpressionParser, and then we inherit from\\nit to create an ExpressionInterpreter. Grammars can be large and complex, and represent\\na sizeable investment of effort, so you might want to use the same grammar for multiple\\npurposes. For example, our ExpressionParser might be used for an expression linter, pretty-printer,\\ninterpreter (as we've done), JS compiler (compile to JS syntax), Java compiler, etc.\\n\\nAs an example, imagine you were writing a tool that linted both EcmaScript versions 14 and 15.\\nThese too languages are mostly the same, but not identical. You could write two separate\\nparsers and linters, or you could use inheritance and composition to reuse most of the code,\\nkeeping your codebase smaller and more maintainable. And then when the next version of\\nEcmaScript comes, your could quickly add support by reusing the code and parsers you already have.\\n\\nThis example is also interesting because it demonstrates a common use of parsers, which is\\ncreating languages. Implementing a new language might seem like some kind of\\ndeep magic, but as you can see in this example, the basic concept isn't difficult.\\nWhile we just create a simple expression interpreter, the same techniques\\ncould be used to make a full fledged language interpreter.\\n</pre>\\n<example>\\n\\nconst input = [\\n  '123',       // number\\n  '-123',      // number\\n  '(123)',     // group\\n  'sqrt(9)',   // fun\\n  'log2(64)',  // fun\\n  'x',         // variable\\n  'y',         // variable\\n  'z',         // variable\\n  '1+2',       // expr\\n  '1+2+3',     // expr\\n  '1*2',       // expr1\\n  '2^4',       // expr2\\n  '1*(x+y*sqrt(9))*-23/4',\\n  'abc'        // bad input\\n];\\n\\n// A simple parser for negative or positive integers\\nfoam.CLASS({\\n  name: 'NumberParser',\\n  extends: 'foam.parse.Grammar',\\n\\n  methods: [\\n    function grammar(plus, sym, alt, seq1, range, str) { return {\\n      START: alt(sym('positive'), sym('negative')),\\n\\n      positive: str(plus(range('0', '9'))),\\n\\n      negative: seq1(1, '-', sym('positive'))\\n    }; },\\n\\n    function positiveAction(s) { return parseInt(s); },\\n\\n    function negativeAction(n) { return -n; }\\n  ]\\n});\\n\\n\\n// A generic Expression Parser. It will just tell you if the expression is valid,\\n// but does not do anything to evaluate it.\\nfoam.CLASS({\\n  name: 'ExpressionParser',\\n  extends: 'foam.parse.Grammar',\\n\\n  requires: [ 'NumberParser' ],\\n\\n  methods: [\\n    function grammar(plus, sym, alt, opt, seq, seq1, lit, range, eof) { return {\\n      START: seq1(0, sym('expr'), eof()),\\n\\n      expr: seq(sym('expr1'), opt(seq(alt('+', '-'), sym('expr')))),\\n\\n      expr1: seq(sym('expr2'), opt(seq(alt('*', '/'), sym('expr1')))),\\n\\n      expr2: seq(sym('expr3'), opt(seq1(1, '^', sym('expr2')))),\\n\\n      expr3: alt(\\n        sym('fun'),\\n        sym('variable'),\\n        sym('number'),\\n        sym('group')),\\n\\n      variable: alt('x', 'y', 'z'),\\n\\n      fun: seq(\\n        alt('sqrt', 'log2'), '(', sym('expr'), ')'\\n      ),\\n\\n      group: seq1(1, '(', sym('expr'), ')'),\\n\\n      number: this.NumberParser.create()\\n    }; }\\n  ]\\n});\\n\\nconst p = ExpressionParser.create();\\n\\ntestP(p, input);\\n\\n// An Expression interpreter. Extends the parser above to implement an interpreter/calculator\\n// which evaluates the supplied expressions.\\nfoam.CLASS({\\n  name: 'ExpressionInterpreter',\\n  extends: 'ExpressionParser',\\n\\n  properties: [ 'x', 'y', 'z' ],\\n\\n  methods: [\\n    function exprAction(v) {\\n      if ( v[1] === null ) return v[0];\\n      if ( v[1][0] == '+' ) return v[0] + v[1][1];\\n      return v[0] - v[1][1];\\n    },\\n    function expr1Action(v) {\\n      if ( v[1] === null ) return v[0];\\n      if ( v[1][0] == '*' ) return v[0] * v[1][1];\\n      return v[0] / v[1][1];\\n    },\\n    function expr2Action(v) {\\n      if ( v[1] === null ) return v[0];\\n      return Math.pow(v[0], v[1]);\\n    },\\n    function funAction(v) {\\n      return v[0] == 'sqrt' ?\\n        Math.sqrt(v[2]) :\\n        Math.log2(v[2]) ;\\n    },\\n    function variableAction(v) { return this[v]; }\\n  ]\\n});\\n\\nconst interp = ExpressionInterpreter.create({x: 1, y: 10, z: 100});\\n\\ntestP(interp, input);\\n\\n// We could extend ExpressionParser again to implement a pretty-printer or a compiler</example>\\n<grammar input=\\"3.14159-26b\\">\\n{\\n  START: repeat(sym('component'), optional(sym('separator'))),\\n  component: alt(sym('number'), sym('string')),\\n  digit: range('0', '9'),\\n  number: str(plus(sym('digit'))),\\n  string: str(plus(not(alt(sym('digit'), sym('separator')), anyChar()))),\\n  separator: alt('.', '-')\\n}\\n</grammar>\\n\\n\\n## Another Reuse Example\\nImagine we wanted to create a calculator for working with binary numbers. Rather than\\nstarting from scratch, we can reuse our existing ExpressionInterpreter and just swap\\nout the default decimal NumberParser with a new BinaryNumberParser.\\n<example>\\nconst input = [\\n  '1',         // number\\n  '0',         // number\\n  '-101',      // number\\n  '(101)',     // group\\n  '1+10',      // addition\\n  '10*100',    // addition\\n  '101+111+1'  // addition\\n];\\n\\nfoam.CLASS({\\n  name: 'BinaryNumberParser',\\n  extends: 'foam.parse.Grammar',\\n\\n  methods: [\\n    function grammar(plus, sym, alt, seq1, range, str) { return {\\n      START: alt(sym('positive'), sym('negative')),\\n\\n      positive: str(plus(range('0', '1'))),\\n\\n      negative: seq1(1, '-', sym('positive'))\\n    }; },\\n\\n    function positiveAction(s) { return parseInt(s, 2); },\\n\\n    function negativeAction(n) { return -n; }\\n  ]\\n});\\n\\nfoam.CLASS({\\n  name: 'BinaryInterpreter',\\n  extends: 'ExpressionInterpreter',\\n  requires: [ 'BinaryNumberParser as NumberParser' ]\\n});\\n\\nconst interp = BinaryInterpreter.create({x: 1, y: 10, z: 100});\\ntestP(interp, input);</example>\\n\\n## Error Reporting\\n<pre>\\nAll of the examples until now have simply returned undefined if the input could not be\\nparsed, but it would be much better to be able to report an error message which\\nexplained why the input could not be parsed. We do this with the ErrorReportingPStream.\\n\\nRecall that PStream is an interface, but notice that the StringPStream is the\\nonly implementation that we've seen so far. The ErrorReportingPStream is a PStream\\ndecorator that wraps another PStream (probably a StringPStream in JS) and figures\\nout where a parse error occurred and provides an error message explaining where the\\nparse failed and what was expected.\\n</pre>\\n<example>\\n// TODO: The JS ErrorReportingPStream isn't as useful as the Java version and should be improved\\n// TODO: finish this example\\n/* unfinished class, breaking the page, temporarily commenting out\\nvar p   = ExpressionParser.create();\\nvar sps = P.stringPStream('1+2*(1+2'); // Missing closing ')'\\n\\nlog('StringPStream:', p.parse(sps));\\n\\nvar erps = foam.parse.ErrorReportingPStream.create({\\n  delegate: P.stringPStream('1+2*(1+2')\\n});\\n\\nvar res = p.parse(erps, p);\\nlog('ErrorReportingPStream:', res);\\nlog(erps);\\n*/</example>\\n"
  },
